{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"resolvable-peridot-wolverine","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Evidence — GitHub Actions run 30604756524\n\n    ERROR at setup of test_add_label_and_the_links_surface_against_a_real_instance\n    AssertionError: PAT creation failed: 403\n      {'exception': 'com.atlassian.pats.exception.UserTokenLimitExceededException',\n       'error': \"You can't create more than 10 tokens. Delete one of your existing tokens\n                 to create a new one.\"}\n\n## Cause, and my part in it\n\n`jira_dc_pat` (`tests/external/live_jira_dc/conftest.py:355`) is declared `@pytest.fixture` with NO\nscope, so it is FUNCTION-scoped: every test that requests it mints a BRAND NEW Personal Access Token\nvia `POST /rest/pat/latest/tokens`, and never deletes it. Jira Data Center caps a user at 10 PATs.\n\nThe fixture also has no teardown — unlike `jira_dc_project` and `track_issue`, which both clean up\nafter themselves. So token consumption grows monotonically across the session.\n\n**Story J7 [rebar:b4e3-1de0-3188-40a1] is what tipped it over.** Its new\n`test_reconcile_pass.py` adds three more tests that transitively request `jira_dc_pat` (via the\n`dc_rebar_repo` fixture), pushing the session past 10. The latent bug was always there; the added\ntests made it fire. Any further live test added to this tier will make it worse.\n\nNote the failure mode is an ERROR AT SETUP of an UNRELATED, previously-passing test\n(`test_transport.py::test_add_label_and_the_links_surface_against_a_real_instance`) — so the symptom\nappears in a module nobody touched, which is exactly the kind of misattribution that wastes a\ndebugging session.\n\n## Fix options (needs a judgement call, not a mechanical change)\n\n* Make `jira_dc_pat` SESSION-scoped — one token for the whole run. Simplest, and the token is\n  already identical for every consumer. Risk: tests that want to prove per-token behaviour would\n  need their own fixture.\n* Or keep it function-scoped and DELETE the token in teardown\n  (`DELETE /rest/pat/latest/tokens/{id}`), mirroring how `jira_dc_project` tears down. Preserves\n  isolation, costs an API call per test.\n* Either way, consider a session-start sweep of leftover `rebar-j5-harness-*` tokens, mirroring the\n  existing `_leaked_scratch_projects` helper, so a previously-crashed run cannot poison the next.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] the full `tests/external/live_jira_dc` suite runs without any `UserTokenLimitExceededException`,\n      with MORE live tests present than today (i.e. headroom, not a coincidence of current count)\n- [ ] a leftover token from a crashed run does not break the next run\n- [ ] whichever approach is chosen is documented in the fixture docstring, since the 10-token cap is\n      a non-obvious Jira DC limit","id":"f391-806b-938f-45d8","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"live_jira_dc harness exhausts Jira DC's 10-PAT-per-user cap; jira_dc_pat is function-scoped"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785473553328065001,"uuid":"cf81cad4-7ace-4b09-9c04-27ce763994f6"}